---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Gather',
    tool: 'HTTP requests to Jina Reader',
    does: 'Fetches each documentation page as plain text (up to 15,000 characters per page). If fewer than two pages are usable, the run stops.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Synthesize',
    tool: 'Claude Haiku 4.5',
    does: 'Extracts up to 25 facts as JSON, each with its source and a verbatim quote, plus any conflicts between sources and any gaps in what they cover.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Draft',
    tool: 'Claude Haiku 4.5',
    does: 'Writes the brief from the fact sheet only, citing a fact for every claim and staying within the chosen word limit.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Review & Format',
    tool: 'Claude Haiku 4.5',
    does: 'Checks every claim against the fact sheet, rewrites or flags overclaims, converts citations to sources and publishes the brief to Google Docs.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Human review',
    tool: 'Me',
    does: 'Every brief stops here. I check the cited sources and record a decision (approve, edit or reject) and the time spent in a Google Sheets run log.',
  },
];

const runs = [
  { topic: 'SQLite write-ahead logging', test: 'Normal case', runtime: '47.1 s', review: '24 min' },
  { topic: 'Docker multi-stage builds', test: 'Normal case', runtime: '32.9 s', review: '23 min' },
  { topic: 'Running asyncio tasks concurrently', test: 'One dead source URL', runtime: '42.0 s', review: '27 min' },
  { topic: 'Pydantic custom field validators', test: 'Sources that conflict', runtime: '49.8 s', review: '29 min' },
  { topic: 'HTTP caching with Cache-Control', test: 'Different audience and word limit', runtime: '44.2 s', review: '25 min' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Tech Docs Research Workflow | Anas Mansour"
  description="Case study: a Make.com and Claude workflow that turns a technical question and 2–5 documentation links into a cited brief, with mandatory human review. Inputs, steps and results from five recorded test runs."
>
  <article class="case container">
    <p class="back"><a href="/#projects">← Back to Selected Projects</a></p>
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study · FlyRank AI internship</p>
    <h1>Tech Docs Research Workflow</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A technical question and 2–5 documentation links go in; a short brief comes out, where every claim
      cites its source and anything uncertain is left visible for a human to check.
    </p>

    <dl class="summary">
      <div>
        <dt>Problem</dt>
        <dd>
          Answering a technical question means reading several documentation pages. AI can speed this up,
          but its answers cannot be trusted unless every claim can be traced back to a source.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>What I built</dt>
        <dd>
          A four-step automated workflow in Make.com using Claude Haiku 4.5, Google Sheets and Google Docs,
          followed by a mandatory human review step.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Result</dt>
        <dd>
          5 of 5 recorded test runs completed end to end with no errors, retries or manual steps, in 32.9–49.8
          seconds of automated runtime each. I reviewed all five briefs and edited every one before use.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
      <h2 id="how-title">How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Each run takes a topic, a question, an audience, 2–5 documentation links and a word limit (300, 500 or
        800). Every step writes its output and status to a Google Sheets run log, so each run can be traced.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        {steps.map((step, i) => (
          <li>
            <p class="step-name"><span class="step-number">{i + 1}</span> {step.name}</p>
            <p class="step-tool">{step.tool}</p>
            <p>{step.does}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="example-title">
      <h2 id="example-title">A real run: HTTP caching</h2>
      <p>The fifth recorded test run, as it was submitted:</p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div>
          <dt>Question</dt>
          <dd>
            Which Cache-Control directives should an API use to control caching, and how do no-cache,
            no-store and max-age differ?
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Audience · word limit</dt>
          <dd>Tech lead · 300 words</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Sources</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Cache-Control" rel="noopener" target="_blank">
              MDN: Cache-Control header<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
            </a><br />
            <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Caching" rel="noopener" target="_blank">
              MDN: HTTP caching guide<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
            </a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <h3>What the workflow produced</h3>
      <ul class="bullets">
        <li>A fact sheet of 22 facts drawn from both sources, with 4 gaps the sources did not answer.</li>
        <li>A 298-word brief (limit 300) that kept its caveats and all 4 open questions.</li>
        <li>
          The automated review rewrote one overstated phrase ("ensuring freshness on each access") and flagged
          the advice to "use max-age alone for public data" for a human to check.
        </li>
        <li>Automated runtime: 44.2 seconds.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>What my review caught (26 September 2026)</h3>
      <ul class="bullets">
        <li>
          The automated review's log said the max-age sentence was "kept but flagged", but the final brief had
          removed it, so the log and the output disagreed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Two recommendations, combining caching approaches and the mention of CDNs, were not in the extracted
          facts and had not been flagged. I marked them for verification and kept the supported Cache-Control
          explanation.
        </li>
        <li>Decision: edited. Review time: about 25 minutes (my own estimate, not timed).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Corrected against the sources (16 October 2026)</h3>
      <p>
        Before publishing this example I checked every claim in the original output against the two MDN pages.
        These corrections were made outside the workflow; the workflow did not make them.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Original output</th><th scope="col">Reviewed version</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>no-cache must be revalidated "before each reuse, even offline"</td>
              <td>"…even when the cache is disconnected from the origin server"</td>
              <td>MDN's wording; "offline" could be read as the user's device being offline</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>no-store "prevents any cache from storing the response"</td>
              <td>"tells caches of any kind … not to store the response"</td>
              <td>MDN says caches "should not store this response": an instruction, not a guarantee</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>"For sensitive data that must not persist, use no-store"</td>
              <td>"If a response should not be stored in any cache, use no-store"</td>
              <td>Neither source mentions sensitive data</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>s-maxage: "combine both when client and intermediate caching differ"</td>
              <td>Removed</td>
              <td>Neither source recommends combining s-maxage with max-age</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>s-maxage "overrides max-age for proxies and CDNs"</td>
              <td>Kept, as shared caches "(for example a proxy or CDN)"</td>
              <td>The Cache-Control page names proxies and CDNs as shared caches</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="/projects/tech-docs/http-caching-brief-reviewed.pdf">
          Read the full reviewed brief (PDF, 2 pages, 116 KB)
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="runs-title">
      <h2 id="runs-title">All five recorded runs</h2>
      <p>
        Each test input was approved before running and run once. Runtime is the automated part only; review
        time is my own whole-minute estimate.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="runs">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Runtime</th><th scope="col">My review</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {runs.map((run) => (
              <tr><td>{run.topic}</td><td>{run.test}</td><td>{run.runtime}</td><td>{run.review}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        All five briefs needed edits, which is why the human review step is mandatory. Problems the automated
        steps missed and my review caught included citations pointing to the wrong source (Pydantic), a dead
        link that was counted as usable (asyncio) and a key page that yielded no facts (Docker).
      </p>
      <p>
        On the one topic I also researched by hand (SQLite), doing it manually took 28 min 39 s; the workflow plus
        my review took about 24 min 47 s. Review time, not the automation, is now the limit.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .case {
    max-width: 46rem;
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .back {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 3rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2.8vw, 1.2rem);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3.5vw, 1.5rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .summary,
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .summary dt,
  .facts dt {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .summary dd,
  .facts dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }

  .facts dd a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .steps li {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .steps li p {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
  }

  .steps li .step-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .step-number {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 1.6rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .steps li .step-tool {
    margin: 0.1rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .bullets {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .bullets li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.55rem 0.6rem;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  th {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .runs td:nth-child(3),
  .runs td:nth-child(4) {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
